    detailedExplanation: "Article 16 is the epicenter of India's affirmative action jurisprudence in public administration.\n\n### 1. Indra Sawhney v. Union of India (1992) (The Mandal Case)\nA 9-judge Constitution Bench established the authoritative constitutional charter on reservations:\n- **Relationship between 16(1) and 16(4):** Article 16(4) is not an exception to 16(1), but an instance of classification to achieve substantive equality.\n- **Backwardness Criteria:** Backward classes under 16(4) are primarily socially and educationally backward; caste can be a starting point of identification, but not the sole factor.\n- **The Creamy Layer Rule:** Socially advanced persons within backward classes ('creamy layer') must be excluded from reservation benefits to ensure gains reach the genuinely deprived.\n- **The 50% Ceiling Limit:** Total reservations under Article 16 cannot exceed 50% of available seats in a year, except in extraordinary regional circumstances.\n- **No Reservation in Promotions:** Article 16(4) applies only to initial appointments, not promotions.\n\n### 2. Parliamentary Overrides: The Constitutional Amendments\nParliament enacted a series of constitutional amendments to dilute aspects of Indra Sawhney:\n1. **77th Amendment (1995) & 85th Amendment (2001) — Article 16(4A):** Authorized reservations in promotions with consequential seniority for SCs and STs.\n2. **81st Amendment (2000) — Article 16(4B):** Exempted unfilled backlog vacancies from the 50% ceiling rule.\n3. **M. Nagaraj v. Union of India (2006):** A 5-judge bench upheld the validity of Articles 16(4A) and 16(4B), but introduced three mandatory constitutional requirements before granting promotion quotas: (a) Quantifiable data showing backwardness; (b) Quantifiable data showing inadequacy of representation; (c) Compliance with administrative efficiency under Article 335.\n4. **Jarnail Singh v. Lachhmi Narain Gupta (2018):** Modified Nagaraj: held that States need NOT collect quantifiable data on backwardness for SC/STs (presumed backward), but held that the **creamy layer principle applies to SCs and STs** in promotion reservations.",
