    detailedExplanation: "Article 19 represents the charter of civil liberties in India.\n\n### 1. Freedom of Speech & Expression (Article 19(1)(a))\n- **Includes Unenumerated Facets:** Freedom of the press (*Romesh Thappar*), right to information (*State of U.P. v. Raj Narain*), right to broadcast (*Cricket Association of Bengal*), commercial speech/advertising (*Tata Press*), right to silence (*Bijoe Emmanuel*), and the right to access the internet (*Anuradha Bhasin*).\n- **Shreya Singhal v. Union of India (2015):** The Supreme Court struck down Section 66A of the Information Technology Act, 2000, establishing the distinction between **discussion**, **advocacy**, and **incitement**. Mere discussion or advocacy of an unpopular cause cannot be restricted; speech can only be curtailed when it reaches the threshold of clear and present incitement to public disorder.\n- **Anuradha Bhasin v. Union of India (2020):** Following the abrogation of Article 370 in J&K, the Court held that the freedom to access information and carry on trade over the internet is protected under Articles 19(1)(a) and 19(1)(g). Indefinite internet suspension orders are illegal; orders must be published and comply with proportionality.\n\n### 2. The Reasonable Restriction Standard\nIn *Chintaman Rao v. State of M.P. (1951)*, the Supreme Court held:\n> *'The phrase 'reasonable restriction' connotes that the limitation imposed on a person in enjoyment of the right should not be arbitrary, or of an excessive nature, beyond what is required in the interests of the public.'*\nRestrictions cannot be based on grounds outside Clauses (2) to (6) (*Sakal Papers v. Union of India*).",
